# What this surface refuses, and the code each refusal carries.
#
# **The refusal list is the feature.** Somebody who writes a join has a question, and "syntax
# error" does not tell them the engine has no joins to give - so every refusal here carries a
# stable code and a sentence saying what exists instead. The codes are what a client branches
# on, so they are what this file pins; the sentences are checked where they are written.
#
# The reasons have collapsed into a few. Rows of a join, window functions, `groupArray`,
# `SELECT *` with values - every one of them asks for **a row**, and a record here is a set of
# bits at `(row, record)` with no row of values to hand back. That is not engineering time; it is
# the shape of the index.
#
# An outer join used to be on that list and is not any more, which is worth saying out loud: it
# reads as a row with half of it nulled out, but what it *is* is a side that stops removing keys
# from the space, and a missing side multiplying by one. That is the same arithmetic the inner
# join already was. What is still refused is a `FULL` mixed with any other kind, because one flag
# per side cannot tell that nesting apart from the union of every side.

## A join is one equality between two keyed columns two tables share.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t, u
----
sql_no_joins

# A cross join and a natural join name no key to pair on, which is what a comma between tables
# is - so they carry that refusal rather than the outer join one, which is about which sides of a
# key space have to match.
error
SELECT count(*) FROM t CROSS JOIN u
----
sql_no_joins

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t NATURAL JOIN u
----
sql_no_joins

# A third table is answered when it is the same star - every table keyed on the one column they
# all share. The *chain*, where one table would have to be grouped by two columns at once, is
# refused as `sql_no_joins` too; it is not recorded here, and `translate/refusals.rs` is where it
# is asserted.

# An `ON` has to name the table it is joining in. Two tables already in scope is a condition
# about neither of the tables this `JOIN` is about, and a table further down the list is a name
# that is not in scope where it stands.
error
SELECT count(*) FROM t JOIN u ON t.category = u.category JOIN v ON t.category = u.category
----
sql_join_condition

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t JOIN u ON t.category = v.category JOIN v ON t.category = v.category
----
sql_join_condition

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t JOIN u ON t.category = u.category FULL JOIN v ON t.category = v.category
----
sql_no_outer_joins

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t JOIN u ON t.category = u.category AND t.country = u.country
----
sql_join_condition

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t JOIN u USING (category)
----
sql_join_condition

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t JOIN u ON t.category > u.category
----
sql_join_condition

## With a join in the statement every column has to say which table it belongs to: the
## translation resolves names before it ever sees a schema, so there is nothing here to guess
## with.

error
SELECT sum(amount) FROM t JOIN u ON t.category = u.category
----
sql_ambiguous_column

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t a JOIN u b ON a.category = b.category WHERE amount > 5
----
sql_ambiguous_column

error
SELECT sum(x.amount) FROM t a JOIN u b ON a.category = b.category
----
sql_ambiguous_column

## A predicate over a join has to be answerable on one side: the two sides are counted
## separately and only meet per key.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t a JOIN u b ON a.category = b.category WHERE a.amount > 1 OR b.amount > 1
----
sql_join_filter

## Over a join this surface answers count, sum, min, max, avg and the shared-key count. What it
## has no answer for is a *row* of one: a pair of records has no identity this engine stores.

error
SELECT * FROM t a JOIN u b ON a.category = b.category
----
sql_unsupported

## An ordering the answer has no value to sort on.

error
SELECT category, count(*) FROM t GROUP BY category ORDER BY country
----
sql_unsupported_order

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t ORDER BY count(*) DESC
----
sql_unsupported_order

## A projection **can** be ordered now - see `order.test` - and it costs what it costs: the sort
## has to see every row, so the plan reads them all and the `LIMIT` moves out of it. What is
## still refused is an ordering the answer holds nothing to sort by.

## An aggregate beside a projection: the answer carries values per record and no number about
## the set for one of these to name.
error
SELECT amount FROM t ORDER BY count(*)
----
sql_unsupported_order

## A column the projection does not read. Sorting by it would mean a second point read per
## record purely to order by, which is a cost the statement did not ask for.
error
SELECT country FROM t ORDER BY amount
----
sql_unsupported_order

## There is no null here, so there is nothing for `IS NULL` to be true of.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount IS NULL
----
sql_no_nulls

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount IS NOT NULL
----
sql_no_nulls

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount = NULL
----
sql_no_nulls

error
SELECT *, count(*) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

## `UNION` removes duplicate rows, and a row here is a rendered answer rather than a stored
## tuple.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t UNION SELECT count(*) FROM u
----
sql_union

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t UNION DISTINCT SELECT count(*) FROM u
----
sql_union

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t INTERSECT SELECT count(*) FROM u
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t EXCEPT SELECT count(*) FROM u
----
sql_unsupported

## A quantile level is a fraction between zero and one, to three digits - which is the
## resolution the search works in. `0` and `1` are the ends of that range and are accepted; a
## whole number that is neither is not a fraction at all.

error
SELECT quantile(1.5)(amount) FROM t
----
sql_quantile_level

error
SELECT quantile(2)(amount) FROM t
----
sql_quantile_level

error
SELECT quantile(0.5555)(amount) FROM t
----
sql_quantile_level

error
SELECT quantile(amount) FROM t
----
sql_quantile_level

## Writes this surface does not take, and the statements that are not questions.

error
UPDATE t SET amount = 1
----
sql_read_only

error
DELETE FROM t WHERE amount > 5
----
sql_read_only

error
TRUNCATE TABLE t
----
sql_read_only

## `INSERT ... SELECT` reads a **projection** - that form is answered, see `insert.test`. What
## is refused is every other shape of answer, because a count or a grouping is a number *about*
## a set of records rather than records to copy.

error
INSERT INTO t (amount) SELECT count(*) FROM u
----
sql_unsupported

error
INSERT INTO t (country) SELECT country FROM u GROUP BY country
----
sql_unsupported

## `SELECT *` answers with record ids, because a record has no row of values to read out.

error
INSERT INTO t (amount) SELECT * FROM u
----
sql_unsupported

## The record ids are allocated, so the column list may not name one: `SELECT _record_id` is
## not a projection this engine has.

error
INSERT INTO t (_record_id, amount) SELECT _record_id, amount FROM u
----
sql_unsupported

## Reading and writing one table in a single statement has no snapshot under it: the records
## being written are visible to the read that is still running.

error
INSERT INTO t (amount) SELECT amount FROM t
----
sql_insert_self_read

## An `INSERT` whose tuples do not match its column list.

error
INSERT INTO t (_record_id, country) VALUES (1, 'GB', 2)
----
parse_error

error
INSERT INTO t (_record_id, country) VALUES (1)
----
parse_error

error
INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'GB')
----
sql_insert_shape

error
INSERT INTO t (_record_id) VALUES ('seven')
----
sql_insert_shape

error
INSERT INTO t (_record_id) VALUES (1.5)
----
sql_insert_shape

error
INSERT INTO t (_record_id) VALUES (-1)
----
sql_insert_shape

## The reserved record column cannot be declared as a field: an `INSERT` could never fill it.

error
CREATE TABLE t (_record_id UINT(32))
----
sql_id_column

error
CREATE TABLE t (a SET, _RECORD_ID INT)
----
sql_id_column

error
ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN _record_id UINT(32)
----
sql_id_column


## Views and the objects this engine does not have.
##
## Databases are no longer among them - see `database.test`. What is still refused is a
## *session*: `POST /sql` answers one statement and keeps nothing, so the database is per
## request rather than something `USE` can leave behind.

error
USE sales
----
sql_use_unsupported

## A column is qualified by an alias and nothing else, so a third part is one name too many.
## Two would make `a.b` ambiguous between "alias a, column b" and "database a, table b", and
## the select list is parsed before `FROM` - so there is nothing here to disambiguate with.

error
SELECT sales.orders.amount FROM sales.orders
----
sql_ambiguous_column

error
SELECT count(*) FROM sales.orders.extra
----
sql_ambiguous_column

## A plain view is answered now - see `view.test`. What is still refused is the materialised
## half, which is not a name for a statement but a table plus a promise to keep it current.

error
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW v AS SELECT count(*) FROM t
----
sql_no_materialized_views

error
DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW v
----
sql_no_materialized_views

error
ALTER MATERIALIZED VIEW v REFRESH
----
sql_no_materialized_views

## `ALTER VIEW` is not among them either, and gets the ordinary write refusal: a view carries a
## name and a statement, and `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW` says the statement - so there is nothing
## left for an `ALTER` to reach.

error
ALTER VIEW v AS SELECT a FROM t
----
sql_read_only

error
CREATE INDEX i ON t (a)
----
sql_read_only

error
SHOW INDEX FROM t
----
parse_error

## A field's bit planes *are* its depth and its kind decides how every fact in it was routed, so
## altering either would mean rewriting every fact ever written to it.

error
ALTER TABLE t MODIFY a BIGINT
----
sql_no_alter_column

error
ALTER TABLE t MODIFY COLUMN a BIGINT
----
sql_no_alter_column

error
ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN a TYPE BIGINT
----
sql_no_alter_column

error
ALTER TABLE t CHANGE a b BIGINT
----
sql_no_alter_column

error
ALTER TABLE t RENAME TO u
----
sql_no_rename

error
ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN a TO b
----
sql_no_rename

error
ALTER TABLE t ENGINE = columnar
----
sql_no_alter_engine

# A database carries a name and nothing else, so there is nothing about one to alter.
error
ALTER DATABASE d RENAME TO e
----
sql_read_only

## Constraints, and the widths that are not bit depths.

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT NOT NULL)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT NULL)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT DEFAULT 0)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a TEXT COMMENT 'why')
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT PRIMARY KEY)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT UNIQUE)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT CHECK (a > 0))
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT AUTO_INCREMENT)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT REFERENCES u (b))
----
sql_no_constraints

error
ALTER TABLE t ADD PRIMARY KEY (a)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
ALTER TABLE t ADD CONSTRAINT c CHECK (a > 0)
----
sql_no_constraints

error
ALTER TABLE t ADD INDEX i (a)
----
sql_no_constraints

# A length on a key bounds nothing: keys are stored whole, with nothing to truncate.
error
CREATE TABLE t (a VARCHAR(255))
----
sql_no_constraints

# A display width is not a bit depth. `INT(11)` is eleven digits where it was written and would
# be eleven bits here, which stops at 2047 - so it is refused, not reinterpreted.
error
CREATE TABLE t (a INT(11))
----
sql_unknown_column_type

error
CREATE TABLE t (a BIGINT(20))
----
sql_unknown_column_type

## Types with no field kind behind them.

## A width in brackets on a type whose name already carries one. `FLOAT(10, 2)` is the one
## worth keeping a case for: it parses in every other dialect and means a fixed-point column
## there, so reading it as a float would silently drop the digits it was asked to keep.

error
CREATE TABLE t (a FLOAT(10, 2))
----
sql_unknown_column_type

error
CREATE TABLE t (a DATETIME(3))
----
sql_unknown_column_type

error
CREATE TABLE t (a JSON)
----
sql_unknown_column_type

error
CREATE TABLE t (a BLOB)
----
sql_unknown_column_type

error
CREATE TABLE t (a BLOB)
----
sql_unknown_column_type

error
CREATE TABLE t (a UUID)
----
sql_unknown_column_type

## A decimal's scale and a field's depth, both decided while the statement is parsed.

error
CREATE TABLE t (a DECIMAL)
----
sql_decimal_scale

error
CREATE TABLE t (a NUMERIC)
----
sql_decimal_scale

error
CREATE TABLE t (a DECIMAL(10))
----
sql_decimal_scale

error
CREATE TABLE t (a DECIMAL(2, 5))
----
sql_decimal_scale

error
CREATE TABLE t (a DECIMAL(25, 2))
----
sql_bit_depth

error
CREATE TABLE t (a UINT(0))
----
sql_bit_depth

error
CREATE TABLE t (a UINT(65))
----
sql_bit_depth

error
CREATE TABLE t (a SIGNED(65))
----
sql_bit_depth

## Expressions **are** answered now - see `scalar.test`. What is left here is the boundary:
## an expression is over one column, and a conversion that would have to invent a value is
## still a conversion this engine cannot do.

## Two columns in one cell is two plans, and a projection reads one field per column.

error
SELECT concat(country, tier) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

## An expression over no column at all is a constant, which needs no table to be true.

error
SELECT 1 + 1 FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

## Widening a day count to an instant has to invent a time of day, and midnight is a guess
## rather than an answer.

error
SELECT toDateTime(visit) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

## The simple form of a case is the searched one with the comparison factored out, and there is
## one spelling of it.

error
SELECT CASE amount WHEN 5 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

## A scalar in a `WHERE` runs before there is anything to run it on - unchanged by the select
## list gaining an evaluator.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE round(amount, 2) > 5
----
sql_scalar_in_filter

## `LIKE` and `ILIKE` are answered - a keyed column interns each string once, so a pattern is a
## walk of that dictionary and a union of what matched. What is still refused is every other
## string comparison, because none of them is a set operation this index can do.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE country SIMILAR TO 'G%'
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE country GLOB 'G*'
----
sql_unsupported

## Aggregates that need values this index does not keep.

error
SELECT stddevPop(amount) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT corr(amount, price) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT argMax(amount, price) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT any(amount) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT groupArray(amount) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported


## Rows, in every spelling.

error
SELECT row_number() OVER () FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) OVER (PARTITION BY category) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT DISTINCT ON (category) category FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

## A subquery, and a `WITH` whose body is a select.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount IN (SELECT amount FROM u)
----
sql_unsupported

error
WITH x AS (SELECT 1) SELECT count(*) FROM t
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) FROM (SELECT * FROM t)
----
parse_error

## A `HAVING` naming a number the answer does not hold, and the offset a grouped answer cannot
## take. An ungrouped `HAVING` over an aggregate the select list *did* ask for is answered - see
## `select.test` - but a projection and a record listing hold no aggregate for one to be about.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t HAVING sum(amount) > 5
----
sql_unsupported

## A comparison between two constants says nothing about any group - it is true or false for
## every one of them alike, and never what anybody meant.

error
SELECT category, count(*) FROM t GROUP BY category HAVING 1 > 2
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT amount FROM t HAVING count(*) > 5
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT * FROM t HAVING count(*) > 5
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t LIMIT 1 OFFSET 5
----
sql_unsupported

## A statement asking more questions than one round of fan-out will carry.

error
SELECT sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 0), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 1), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 2), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 3), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 4), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 5), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 6), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 7), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 8), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 9), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 10), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 11), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 12), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 13), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 14), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 15), sum(amount) FILTER (WHERE amount > 16) FROM t
----
sql_too_many_aggregates

## An output format this surface does not write.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t FORMAT Arrow
----
sql_unknown_format

error
SHOW TABLES FORMAT Parquet
----
sql_unknown_format

## Text that is not a statement.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM
----
parse_error

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount >
----
parse_error

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE country = 'unterminated
----
parse_error

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t LIMIT 5 trailing
----
parse_error

error
CREATE TABLE t (a)
----
parse_error

error
CREATE TABLE t (SET)
----
parse_error

error
DROP TABLE
----
parse_error

error
DROP TABLE a, b
----
parse_error

error
SHOW
----
parse_error

error
DESCRIBE
----
parse_error

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount > 99999999999999999999999
----
parse_error

## Nested past the depth the recursive parser will take, which is a limit rather than a
## judgement about the query.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE ((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((((amount > 1))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
----
query_too_deep

## The scalar calls, and the two ways of asking for one that cannot be answered.

## A boundary the calendar does not have. Refused at parse time, because the list of boundaries
## is the dialect's rather than the schema's.
error
SELECT date_trunc('fortnight', seen) FROM tx
----
sql_bad_trunc_unit

## A rounding in a `WHERE`. It reads a value per record, and a `WHERE` runs over bitmaps before
## there are any values - so the refusal names the range to write instead.
error
SELECT count(*) FROM tx WHERE toDate(seen) = '2024-01-15'
----
sql_scalar_in_filter

error
SELECT count(*) FROM tx WHERE date_trunc('month', seen) = '2024-01-01'
----
sql_scalar_in_filter

## A semi-join narrows one table by the record ids another table's `WHERE` selected. Two things
## about it are refused, and both are about the ids rather than about the syntax.

## The inner select may only ask for `_record_id`: a record id is the only thing one table can
## hold about another's records, and every other select list asks for a number or a value that
## the outer column could not be one of.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount IN (SELECT country FROM u)
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount IN (SELECT _record_id FROM u GROUP BY country)
----
sql_unsupported

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount IN (SELECT _record_id FROM u LIMIT 10)
----
sql_unsupported

## A segment is a named `WHERE` over one table, and expanding one needs the catalog the view is
## stored in. This translation has none - which is what makes every case in this file a parser
## test - so a statement naming a segment is refused here and substituted by a server.

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE SEGMENT(big_spenders)
----
sql_segment_unexpanded

error
SELECT count(*) FROM t WHERE amount > 5 AND NOT SEGMENT(churned)
----
sql_segment_unexpanded
